etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4167402\nCREATED: 2026-06-06\nDOMAIN: nypost.com\nSLUG: usc-baseball-falls-flat-in-super-regional-forcing-decisive-game-3\nTEXT: USC baseball falls flat in Super Regional, forcing decisive Game 3. DeCaro logs first complete-game shutout in a Super Regional elimination game since Vanderbilt\'s Kumar Rocker threw a no-hitter against Duke in 2019. DeCaro stifles an offense that had combined for 64 runs over its previous five games, giving up only two hits and one walk while also hitting a batter. He reaches 95 mph on his fastball, sweating through his jersey in the final innings and striking out eight, including Kevin Takeuchi who chases a third strike on a curveball to end the game.\n\nID: 4167643\nCREATED: 2026-06-07\nDOMAIN: ocregister.com\nSLUG: ncaa-super-regional-north-carolina-shuts-out-usc-forces-deciding-game\nTEXT: North Carolina shuts out USC 4-0 to force a deciding game in the NCAA Super Regional series. Jason DeCaro pitched a complete game, striking out eight and allowing only two hits and one walk. Collin Hynek went 2-for-4 with a home run and two runs scored for North Carolina. USC\'s Grant Grovel gave up a home run to Hynek in the second inning and left the game after five innings trailing 1-0. The deciding game is scheduled for Sunday at noon Pacific Time.\n\nID: 4167403\nCREATED: 2026-06-06\nDOMAIN: nypost.com\nSLUG: uscs-bullpen-cant-repeat-lockdown-effort-in-super-regional\nTEXT: USC\'s bullpen failed to replicate its success from the previous day, allowing three runs in Game 2 of an NCAA Tournament Super Regional against North Carolina. The relievers, who had been a strength just 24 hours earlier, struggled against the fifth-seeded Tar Heels in Chapel Hill. The loss forced a deciding Game 3 on Sunday, as USC fell 4-0 to North Carolina.\n\n\n',)
