etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4167568\nCREATED: 2026-06-07\nDOMAIN: foxnews.com\nSLUG: victor-wembanyama-well-7-feet-tall-gets-away-hard-foul-getting-boxed-jose-alvarado\nTEXT: In the 2026 NBA Finals, Victor Wembanyama, a 7-foot-4 player for the San Antonio Spurs, was ejected from Game 2 after committing a flagrant foul against Knicks guard Jose Alvarado. Wembanyama was boxed out by Alvarado and may have gotten away with hard contact, but he then wrapped his arms around Alvarado\'s neck and tossed him like a rag doll, prompting Wembanyama to ask for a whistle but never receiving one. Despite scoring 22 points in the second half, Wembanyama\'s efforts were not enough to overcome a costly turnover and a missed game-winning basket, as the Knicks secured a 105-104 victory to take a 2-0 lead in the series.\n\nID: 4167567\nCREATED: 2026-06-07\nDOMAIN: foxnews.com\nSLUG: victor-wembanyama-admits-harsh-truths-costly-mistake-end-nba-finals-game\nTEXT: San Antonio Spurs forward Victor Wembanyama admitted to making a costly mistake in the NBA Finals game against the New York Knicks, which led to the Knicks taking a 2-0 series lead. Wembanyama threw away a potential game-winning pass to teammate Stephon Castle, and later missed a jumper that could have tied the game. He also acknowledged being "high" from their previous seven-game Western Conference Finals win against the Oklahoma City Thunder, which may have contributed to his mistake. The Knicks now hold every ounce of momentum heading into Game 3, which is expected to be the most expensive sporting event of all time.\n\nID: 4167399\nCREATED: 2026-06-06\nDOMAIN: nypost.com\nSLUG: new-video-angle-shows-what-victor-wembanyama-saw-on-nba-finals-game-2-turnover\nTEXT: Victor Wembanyama\'s errant pass and foul cost the Spurs in their Game 2 loss to the Knicks. A new video angle shows what Wembanyama saw on the turnover, as he grabbed a defensive rebound with 11.8 seconds remaining and passed to Stephon Castle before throwing the ball off his back, allowing Castle to push the ball up the court. The Spurs had foregone their timeout, and Wembanyama took a step, dribbled, and another step before making the pass. Castle had turned his head forward and was running up court when Wembanyama threw the ball off, costing the Spurs the game.\n\n\n',)
